// DYNAMIC.md §4.7 / §7: assigning to a list element `e[i] = v` writes through
// the index in place. The target is an l-value, so under TS it drops the
// non-null assertion the read position carries (`xs[i] = v`): the slot is
// written, not read, so the `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` guard is dead weight on
// it. Lua adjusts the offset by one; Python grows the list when the
// index is the next slot. A variable index exercises the runtime-resolved
// write. The list starts `[1, 2, 3]` and ends `[10, 20, 30]`, so the golden
// proves the writes landed, not that construction alone produced the values.
extern {
    fn println(n: number);
}

fn main() {
    let xs = [1, 2, 3];
    xs[0] = 10;
    xs[1] = 20;
    let i = 2;
    xs[i] = 30;
    println(xs[0]);
    println(xs[1]);
    println(xs[2]);
}
